# $title

This is your private research book. Agents look for ways to invest in
stocks held $holding. Each idea is written down as a bet before any testing:
the odds, how much to put on it, and the date by which it must prove itself.
The agents do the research and keep the books. You decide, and you place
every trade yourself.

## Your daily brief

Each day the agents work on their own, then send you a short brief in chat
at $when. It has at most twelve lines: the market picture, any proposed
trades with their odds and size, warnings from the last week, and bets that
are past due. Its last line names the one reply it needs from you, or says
that nothing needs deciding.

A proposal waits for your answer. Nothing is traded and no setting changes
until you say so.

## How to reply

Answer in plain words, in chat. There are four kinds of reply.

1. Decide on a proposal. Say approve, reject, or override, with your reason
   in your own words. The agents keep your words exactly as you wrote them.
2. Report what you traded. $sending$account After trading, paste the
   confirmation table from your broker, attach a screenshot, or describe the
   trades in a sentence. The agents show you what they read; say yes if it
   is right.
3. Change a setting. Ask in a sentence, for example a lower risk limit or a
   sector to leave out. The agents show you the change, make it when you say
   yes, and keep your words beside it.
4. Ask anything. The agents answer from the book's history first.

## Where the history is

Everything the agents decided, tested, and traded is kept in the `ledger/`
folder of this book. Lines are only ever added there, never changed, so it
is the full history.
